{"actor":"server","event":"response","topic_id":"2024-42014","model_call":4,"request":{"op":"next","qid":"q1585"},"record":{"query":{"op":"next","qid":"q1585"},"response":{"burrow":"/data/ssd3/claclark/msm-2.1.burrow","docno":"msmarco_v2.1_doc_00_744067170#4_1389605075","ok":true,"op":"next","qid":"q1585","rank":4,"snippet":"likelihood of exclusivity and bullying diminishes. 3. Religious literacy is key to a well-rounded education. If students are to function as globally competent citizens, they need to understand religion’s profound impact on history, politics, society, and culture. They should know basic religious facts and principles and recognize the diversity that exists within each belief system across time and place. Familiarity with central religious texts is also important, and it’s <cover>legal to study these texts in public schools, as long as the purpose is educational and not personal or devotional. For example, the Bible can be studied as a piece of literature that has influenced many classic works. 4. Students have a First Amendment right to religious expression in school. The U.S. Constitution contains two clauses, known as the religion </cover>clauses, which inform the relationship between religion and public schools.\" , \"start_char\": 2703.000000 , \"title\": \"Four Reasons Why You Should Teach About Religion in School (Opinion)\" , \"url\": \"http://blogs.edweek.org/edweek/global_learning/2014/04/four_reasons_why_you_should_teach_about_religion_in_school.html\" }"},"time":9},"model_calls":4,"document_judgments":3,"relevance_3":3,"max_document_judgments":50,"max_model_calls":100}
